




                       Interactive Computer Ephemeris

                          Nautical Almanac Office
                           U.S. Naval Observatory


                                  Addendum
                                     to
                        Floppy Almanac User's Guide

   This addendum to the Floppy Almanac User's Guide describes the changes 
   that  have been made to the Floppy Almanac to produce  its  successor, 
   the Interactive Computer Ephemeris. In this addendum, the abbreviation 
   FA stands for the Floppy Almanac, and the abbreviation ICE stands  for 
   Interactive Computer Ephemeris.

   The  most significant difference between the two programs is the  time 
   span  of the data. The ICE can compute ephemerides for a time span  of 
   approximately 250 years while the time span of the FA is limited to  1 
   year.  This  enormous increase is made possible because the  ICE  uses 
   data  compressed  by such a large factor that only four  360  kilobyte 
   disks  are  required to contain the program and all of the  data  that 
   would have required 250 disks using the FA format. A trade off is made 
   in  the  program speed however; the ICE program runs  slightly  slower 
   than the FA.

   Another change is the name; the program is now called the  Interactive 
   Computer Ephemeris, and the name of the executable file on the disk is 
   ICE.EXE. The overall appearance and operation of the ICE is  unchanged 
   from that of the FA. Changes in the internal structure of the  program 
   have  increased the amount of memory required. Now, a minimum  of  384 
   kilobytes  of RAM are required in a machine running MS-DOS 2.11.  More 
   memory  may be required if you use memory resident programs or have  a 
   later version of DOS.

   A hard disk capable of holding all the Interactive Computer  Ephemeris 
   files in a single directory is recommended, but is NOT required, since 
   the ICE will prompt the user for disk changes. The arrangement of  the 
   files on the 360k disks is listed below. Each pair of disks #1 and #2, 
   and  disks  #3  and  #4 are combined onto  a  single  720k  disk  when 
   distributed on that media.

   Disk #1:  ICE       EXE       Disk #2:  EPH20     DAT
             PARAM     DAT                 EPH21     DAT
             EPH18     DAT                 EPH22     DAT
             EPH19     DAT                 EPH23     DAT
             READ      ME                  EPH24     DAT
                                           CATALOG   EXE
                                           STAR1     CAT
                                           RADIO1    CAT
                                           MESSIER   CAT
                                           SAMPLE    TXT




   Disk #3:  EPH01     DAT       Disk #4:  EPH11     DAT
             EPH02     DAT                 EPH12     DAT
             EPH03     DAT                 EPH13     DAT
             EPH04     DAT                 EPH14     DAT
             EPH05     DAT                 EPH15     DAT
             EPH06     DAT                 EPH16     DAT
             EPH07     DAT                 EPH17     DAT
             EPH08     DAT
             EPH09     DAT
             EPH10     DAT

   If sufficient space is available on a hard disk or in a RAM disk,  all 
   of  the  files may be copied there and the ICE will be  able  to  find 
   whichever files it needs.

   The  Compressed Ephemeris, recently developed at the Nautical  Almanac 
   Office,  enables ephemeris data to be stored in only a small  fraction 
   of  the  space required to store the actual planetary  coordinates.  A 
   brief  description  of  the  internal  operations  of  the  Compressed 
   Ephemeris follows. The stored data are considerably different from the 
   Chebychev  polynomial coefficients of the FA, and a greater amount  of 
   calculation  is  required to extract the  planetary  coordinates.  The 
   stored  data  are  parameters describing a  reference  orbit  and  the 
   Chebychev  polynomials  which represent the  differences  between  the 
   actual  coordinates and the coordinates obtained from the  parameters. 
   The  net  result, however, is the  rectangular  planetary  coordinates 
   required  by  the  ICE program; the details  of  the  calculation  are 
   invisible  to  the  user. The data for the  Compressed  Ephemeris  are 
   stored on disk as 24 separate files, each small enough so that only  a 
   moderate  amount  of  memory is required to hold one  file.  When  the 
   boundary date of a given file is crossed, the ICE program will attempt 
   to  read in the next necessary file. This disk reading requires a  few 
   seconds  and  slows the program down. Once the file has been  read  in 
   calculations will resume.

   If  the expected file is not found in the current directory, an  error 
   message is displayed on the screen, along with a request for the  user 
   to insert the correct disk. The message will appear as:

        Ephemeris file EPH01.DAT not found on active disk.
        Insert correct disk and press "Enter" when ready.

   When  this error message is displayed, the user may swap disks, or  by 
   typing the word "COMMAND" may enter a DOS shell and do any  operations 
   needed (such as copying files from one disk to another). To leave  the 
   DOS  shell,  type the word "EXIT" at the DOS prompt and the  ICE  will 
   reappear  on  the screen. If the needed file is still not  found,  the 
   error  message will be repeated. The ICE program may be terminated  at 
   this  point  by pressing and holding the "Ctrl" key and  pressing  the 
   "Break" key.

   Each  of  the  24 Compressed Ephemeris data files  covers  4000  days, 
   except  the first, EPH01.DAT, and the last, EPH24.DAT, which  cover  a 
   shorter  span. The calendar and Julian dates covered by each file  are 
   listed in the following table.



