        NOTE: The Julian date changes at noon in the civil calendar, 
        i.e.  at  12  noon,  October 1,  1988  the  Julian  date  is 
        2447436.0. The Julian dates given in the table correspond to 
        noon on the calendar dates.]


   This  table  is  provided so that the user will be able  to  copy  the 
   necessary  files  for  the time span of interest to  a  working  disk. 
   EPH10.DAT and EPH19.DAT, covering the present era, are located on  the 
   same  distribution  disk  as the main ICE  program.  When  creating  a 
   working disk, only four files are required. These files are:

        ICE.EXE
        ICE.DFT
        PARAM.DAT
        EPHxx.DAT   (any 1 of the 24 ephemeris files)

   The file ICE.DFT may be omitted, but the standard default location and 
   time  zone  will  be used, unless changed from the main  menu  in  the 
   program.




                               IMPORTANT NOTE
                               --------------

   This  is  a  Beta version of the ICE. That means that it  is  not  yet 
   complete,  but  is ready for extensive testing by a  large  number  of 
   users.  It  also means that the program will be changing in  the  near 
   future. Most of the changes will be cosmetic to fix details that  have 
   been  reported  to  us  by user of the FA.  There  will  be  some  new 
   astronomical  calculations added. Specifically, we intend to  add  the 
   physical ephemeris of the Sun, heliocentric rectangular coordinates of 
   the  planets, topocentric apparent and astrometric positions, and  the 
   ability to compute positions of minor planets and comets from a  table 
   of orbital elements.

   Furthermore,  it  is the intention of the Nautical Almanac  Office  to 
   replace  all of the Beta versions with copies of the finished  product 
   at  no charge to the original purchaser. You will be notified by  mail 
   when the final version is available.


                                   ERRATA
                                   ------

   The  original  issue of this Addendum, as received from  the  Nautical 
   Almanac Office, contained an incorrect conversion from Julian date  to 
   calendar  date  in the table. The calendar date  for  JD2412000.0  was 
   given  as 1889 07 16 and should be 1891 09 24. The two occurrences  of 
   the error have been corrected in this version.

   1989-08-05  Dave Ransom

